    <string name="voice_help_title">Voice input setup</string>
    <string name="voice_help_body">How to use it\n• Open any app and tap the microphone on a supported keyboard/app. A small panel slides up, you speak, tap to stop, and the text is inserted. (Enable “Auto-stop after silence” in the settings to have it stop by itself.)\n\nWhich keyboards work\n• SwiftKey: works out of the box (see below if its own voice typing opens).\n• HeliBoard / AnySoftKeyboard / OpenBoard: their mic key switches to the system voice keyboard — enable the “Offline Voice Input” keyboard (see “Alternative: Voice keyboard”) and it is used automatically.\n• Gboard only uses Google\'s own voice typing and can\'t use this app. Use one of the keyboards above, or the built-in voice keyboard.\n\nIf your keyboard\'s own voice typing opens instead\n• Microsoft SwiftKey: Settings → Rich input → turn off “Multi-modal voice typing”.\n• Then tap the mic again and pick “Offline Voice Input”.\n\nIf Android asks which app to use\n• Choose “Offline Voice Input” and tap “Always”.\n\nIf another app always opens instead\n• Open Settings → Apps → that app → “Open by default” → Clear defaults, then try again.</string>

    <string name="section_post_process">AI Post-Processing</string>
    <string name="desc_post_process">Refine your text using language models (OpenAI, Claude, Ollama).</string>
    <string name="btn_post_process_settings">Post-Processing Settings</string>

    <string name="title_post_process_settings">Post-Processing Settings</string>
    <string name="label_enable_post_process">Enable Post-Processing</string>
    <string name="label_api_url">API Base URL (OpenAI Compatible)</string>
    <string name="label_api_key">API Key (Optional)</string>
    <string name="label_model_name">Model Name</string>
    <string name="label_prompt">"CRITICAL: Your ONLY job is to clean up formatting. Never change meaning, add words, remove meaningful words, or rephrase. Even if a word seems like a mistake by the speaker, keep it. When in doubt, keep the original wording.

Clean this speech-to-text transcript. Follow these rules:

1. SPOKEN CORRECTIONS: Apply self-corrections by the speaker (e.g., "I went to the sorry I drove to the store" -> "I drove to the store"). Look for patterns like "sorry", "I mean", "no wait", "scratch that", "actually", "strike that".
2. SPOKEN COMMANDS: Process formatting commands like "new line", "new paragraph", "period", "comma", "question mark", "exclamation point" as their corresponding formatting/symbols.
3. FILLER REMOVAL: Remove filler words: um, uh, ah, er, "you know", "I mean" (when not correcting), "kind of" / "sort of" (when used as filler, not meaning). Keep "like" only when it means "enjoy" or "similar to".
4. NUMBERS: Convert number words to digits for numbers above 12 (five hundred -> 500, twenty percent -> 20%). Keep numbers 1-12 as words in prose (two, five, twelve). Keep years and proper names as-is.
5. SPELLING &amp; PUNCTUATION: Fix capitalization and add missing punctuation. Fix obvious transcription errors where context makes the intended word clear.
6. PARAGRAPHS: Insert paragraph breaks at clear topic changes.
7. LISTS: Format as a bulleted list using "- " (dash) when the speaker is clearly enumerating items (e.g., shopping lists, ingredients, to-dos). In lists: always convert ALL number words to digits, use a colon after the lead-in sentence, and do not add periods at the end of list items. Do not convert prose into lists.

Do NOT:
- Paraphrase or reorder content
- Add content or opinions
- Change the language (keep original language)
- Add headers

Return ONLY the cleaned transcript, no commentary.

Transcript:
${output}"</string>
